  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 2320071,
  "published": "1900-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## Changes since 0.1.0\n\n### Added\n- `--dry-run` prints the delist plan (would delist / already delisted / not on server) without sending any delete request. No API key required. Exit is bucket-aware: `0` only when every version exists, `1` when any is missing, `2` when the package does not exist.\n- `--output json`: newline-delimited JSON on stdout, one object per version with exactly `package`, `version`, `status` in that order. Stderr stays human-readable.\n- A documented exit-code contract, pinned by tests: `0` success, `1` per-version failure or a command line the parser rejects, `2` a validation failure the CLI detects itself, `3` rate-limited, `4` cancelled, `130` Ctrl-C. The four `return -1` paths are gone.\n- Six-RID release binaries alongside the `dotnet tool` package: `win-x64`, `win-arm64`, `linux-x64`, `linux-arm64`, `osx-x64`, `osx-arm64`. Self-contained single-file, each with a SHA-256 file plus a combined `sha256.txt`. The RID binaries run the HTTP backend with no .NET installed.\n- `DelistOptions`, which resolves option precedence (`--option` then `PRERELEASEDELIST_*` then `NUGET_*` then default) and validates per mode, so dry-run skips the API-key check.\n\n### Changed\n- Updated CliInvoke to 3.0.3 (3.0.0 is affected by [GHSA-h4rx-7jm8-fr3g](https://github.com/alastairlundy/CliInvoke/security/advisories/GHSA-h4rx-7jm8-fr3g)), but PreReleaseDelist never invokes a runner, so the vulnerable code path is unreachable\n- Rate limiting fails fast: dispatching stops, results are reported with the rest marked `not-attempted`, and the process exits `3`. No retry.\n- Results, summaries and dry-run plans go to stdout; errors and diagnostics go to stderr. The API key never reaches either stream.\n- Both backends share one per-version delete path behind a single seam, with `DelistPlanning.Partition` composed in front of it and cross-cutting behaviour installed as decorators.\n- `--non-interactive` lines render `Version=<normalized> Status=<kebab-case>` from a closed vocabulary (`delisted`, `already-delisted`, `not-on-server`, `rate-limited`, `failed`, `not-attempted`). The old `Success`/`Failure` text and its free-text `Info=`/`Error=` fields are gone.\n- The command is a presentation and failure boundary now: option resolution lives in `DelistOptions`, known exceptions become one-line stderr messages, unknown ones still crash with stacks.\n- Shared build policy in `Directory.Build.props` and `.editorconfig`: C# 14, nullable, warnings as errors, style checks at build, NuGet audit. `GeneratePackageOnBuild` is replaced by an explicit `dotnet pack`, so a local `dotnet build` no longer drops a `.nupkg`.\n- Packaging tags corrected, README packed once from the repo root, and README/CHANGELOG rewritten for the new flags, exit codes and both distribution channels.\n- Publish gates pack and push on the test suite, and builds the RID assets only from a green tree.\n- Dependabot now points at `/src` where it actually finds the projects, covers `github-actions` too, groups NuGet minor/patch bumps, and runs weekly with a 14-day version cooldown. Security updates stay immediate.\n- Dropped `EnhancedLinq`, which existed for one `Exclude` call; both services use `DelistPlanning.Partition` now.\n\n### Removed\n- The `IsTrimmable` and `IsAoTCompatible` claims. Trimmed and Native AOT publishes both build and then die at runtime, because `NuGet.Protocol` reads signature resources through Newtonsoft.Json reflection the trimmer deletes. The properties stay out until a publish-and-run against a real feed passes.\n\n### Fixed\n- `--delist-all` works without `versions`. The argument was marked required, so every documented invocation died with `Required argument missing for command`.\n- Versions the server does not know about are failures, not successes. A missing metadata entry was indistinguishable from \"present but unlisted\", so a typo exited 0 with `Status=Success Info='Package already de-listed.'`. Both backends had this.\n- Unknown options are rejected instead of swallowed. The tokenizer turned an unrecognized `--flag` into an argument token, the greedy `versions` position took it, and the digit filter dropped it, so `--dry-runn` fell through to the run it was meant to prevent. The CLI now writes `Error: Unrecognized option: '--flag'.` to stderr and exits `2`. README examples that passed versions as `--versions`, which only worked because that token was ignored, use positional arguments now.\n- Ctrl-C reports `130` again. Neither backend converts a cancellation of the caller token into a `Failed` outcome. A `TaskCanceledException` from something else, like the HttpClient timeout, still maps to `Failed`.\n- The SDK backend matches `429` as a standalone token, so a version number like `2.429.0` in failure output no longer reads as a rate limit.\n- On the `http` backend, a `404` counts as already-delisted only when the service index declared the `PackagePublish/2.0.0` endpoint the delete actually hit. On the fallback route a `404` stays a failure.\n- `--include-zero-major` reaches `CheckPackageVersionsListedAsync`, which used to ignore it.\n- `global.json` says `10.0.100`, not `10.0.0`. Feature band 0 never ships, and setup-dotnet rejects it when `rollForward` is set, which broke the dependency-submission workflow.",
